  "query_preview": "..Water vapor shows a closed low centered over the\nborder of Sonora and Chihuahua and a ridge building in over the\nGreat Basin. The low will move into Texas over the next couple\ndays with the ridge dominating the Southwest for most of the\nwork week. At the surface there is still a moderate pressure\ngradient in place and will lead to continued breezy easterly\nwinds today with some lingering breezes Monday. Speeds will be\nat a maximum this morning into the early afternoon then begin \nto decrease as the gradient weakens. Expect speeds 12-20 mph \nwith gusts 20-30 mph. \n\nDuring the upcoming work week temperatures increase a few\ndegrees as the ridge builds in. Starting Monday high\ntemperatures will be around normal or a couple degrees above\nnormal through at least Friday. Friday into the weekend the \nridge breaks down and ensembles favor a more progressive \npattern. Ensembles show a cutoff low, that pinches off from the\nmain longwave trough over the Gulf of Alaska, and moves into \nCalifornia or Baja Friday into Saturday. This is then followed \nby another low digging south from the Pacific Northwest. Right \nnow there is more uncertainty with the evolution of the second \ntrough, but there is growing confidence in rain chances \nreturning next weekend as the two lows move through. NBM PoPs \nare 20-40 percent late Friday into Sunday with the best chances\nSaturday.",
